<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-electric-cars">
  <title>Charging electric cars</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>"We all make mistakes, and it is better to make them before we begin."</p>
      <p>--- Nikola Tesla</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      A 2014-15 study examined how electric-car drivers used workplace charging facilities provided by a firm in the
      U.S. Department of Energy workplace charging challenge.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Questions.</em> How often did users charge? When did they charge? How long for? Which stations were most
      used? Which incentives controlled station use?
    </p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> Asensio et al. study and companion materials.</p>
    <p>
      <em>Structure.</em> 3,395 charging sessions with 24 variables, including user, location, time, charge, and
      cost.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-elec-study">
    <title>How was the study organised?</title>

    <p>
      The study had a hierarchy of four facility types, multiple locations within each type, and varying numbers of
      stations per location. Distances driven and alternative charging options were not recorded. Financial and other
      incentives were used to reduce congestion, but congestion itself was not measured directly.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecens">
      <image source="images/elecEns-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Number of chargings by user, facility type, location, and charging station (locations and stations are
          coloured by facility type).
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The ensemble shows a few heavy users and many infrequent users. Most locations and stations were at R&amp;D
      facilities, with large variation in usage across both locations and stations.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-eleclocs">
      <image source="images/elecLocs-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Numbers of charging stations by facility type and location.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The 25 locations are sorted by facility type and then by number of stations, making capacity differences easy
      to compare.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecchu">
      <image source="images/elecChU-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Numbers of charges per day with smooth added.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Daily usage over time shows pronounced dips likely corresponding to weekends.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecstatts">
      <image source="images/elecStatts-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Numbers of charging stations by FacilityType over the study.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Stations were introduced over time, so facilities were not observed over equal durations. R&amp;D always had
      the most stations available.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecstat">
      <image source="images/elecStat-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>When charging stations were used during the study.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecu">
      <image source="images/elecU-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>When users charged their cars, sorted by number of charges.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecstat2">
      <image source="images/elecStat2-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Use of charging stations, coloured by facility type, grouped by location, ordered by first installation at
          location and first date of use, with the end of testing marked in red.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The revised display adds location context and highlights imbalance: some locations/stations were barely used,
      while others were heavily used. The practical analysis period appears shorter than the paper's stated nine
      months.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-elec-duration">
    <title>How long does charging cars take?</title>

    <p>
      Charging was free for up to four hours, then $1/hour. Staff were encouraged to keep sessions under two hours.
      Sessions above 55 hours and zero-charge sessions were excluded in the figure.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecch">
      <image source="images/elecCh-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Distribution of charging time in hours.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Usage is concentrated below four hours with no obvious drop at two hours, and a small number of very short
      sessions.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-eleccht">
      <image source="images/elecChT-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Energy charge in kilowatt-hours by charging time in hours.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Charge versus time reveals a strong increasing pattern up to about two hours, then flattening. Some sessions
      look highly efficient, some weak, and a few near-zero for long times.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecchtc">
      <image source="images/elecChTc-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Energy charge in kilowatt-hours by charging time in hours, with charges above 10 kWh coloured by user ID
          (lower charges for those users not recoloured).
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Most very high charges are attributable to a small subset of users, plausibly reflecting different vehicle
      characteristics.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-elec-week">
    <title>Charging patterns through the week</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Grouping charge-time scatterplots by weekday and facility type shows pronounced weekly structure, excluding
        sessions above six hours and the small "Other" group.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-elecscats">
        <image source="images/elecScats-1.png"/>
        <caption>
          <p>
            Energy charge in kilowatt-hours by charging time in hours, grouped by weekday and facility type,
            excluding charging times over six hours. The barchart shows number of charges by day.
          </p>
        </caption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Weekend charging is sparse. R&amp;D accounts for many sessions and somewhat larger weekday charges. Lower
        boundaries suggest a minimum time needed for a given charge. The study excluded 55 non-charging sessions even
        when connection times were non-negligible.
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>Answers.</em> About half of users charged rarely or not at all; charging was uncommon on weekends;
        several locations were lightly used; most sessions stayed under four hours, after which fees began.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Further questions.</em> How did patterns evolve over time? When did users start/stop charging during the
        day? What fraction of time were stations occupied?
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-elec-takeaways">
      <title>Graphical takeaways</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Initial exploratory graphics suggest concrete follow-up questions.</p></li>
        <li><p>Colour, spacing, and nested ordering are powerful for distinguishing groups.</p></li>
        <li><p>Ensembles of graphics are effective for combining complementary information.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
